1.d4 {Notes from "The Chess Amateur", February 1922, and Efim
Bogoljubow.} Nf6 2.Nf3 d5 3.c4 e6 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 Nbd7 6.Nc3
O-O 7.Rc1 Re8 {?!} 8.Qc2 dxc4 {?!} 9.Bxc4 c5 10.O-O cxd4
11.Nxd4 a6 {Bogoljubow: Black should play 11...Ne5 12.Be2 Bd7
13.Rfd1 Qb6 with a satisfactory position.} 12.Rfd1 Qa5 13.Bh4
Ne5 14.Be2 Ng6 15.Bg3 e5 {TCA: If 15...Nh5, then 16.Nb3 Qg5
17.Ne4 with much the better game.} 16.Nb3 Qc7 17.Qb1 {!} Qb8
18.Bf3 Qa7 19.Na5 {! TCA: Stops Black from obtaining a little
more freedom by 19...Rb8 and 20...h5.} Bb4 20.Nc4 Bd7 21.Nd5
Nxd5 22.Bxd5 Be6 23.Qe4 {Bogoljubow: Also good is 23.Nxe5 Nxe5
24.Bxe5 and if 24...Bg4 25.Bd4 with an extra pawn, but
Rubinstein prefers to keep the pressure on.} Bxd5 24.Rxd5 Rac8
25.Rcd1 Bf8 26.b3 b5 27.Nd6 Bxd6 28.Rxd6 Rc7 {? Bogoljubow:
Black misses his last chance: 28...Qa8!. Now 29.R6d5 is bad
because of 29...f5! 30.Qxf5 Qxd5 31.Rxd5 Rc1+. If 29.Qf5 then
29...f6 30.h4 Nh8 is possible and Black's position is
playable.} 29.h4 {!} f6 30.Qd5+ Kh8 31.h5 Nf8 32.h6 Ng6 33.Qe6
{!} Rf8 34.Rd7 gxh6 35.Bh4 {! TCA: For if 35...Nxh4 36.Qe7
wins.} 1-0

